Wiring:
- DHT22 module VCC -> ESP32 3V3.
- DHT22 module DATA -> GPIO4.
- DHT22 module GND -> ESP32 GND.
- GPIO23 -> 220 ohm resistor -> LED anode.
- LED cathode -> GND.

Because the DHT22 module already includes its DATA pull-up, do not add another resistor. Powering the module from 3.3 V keeps the DATA high level within the ESP32's 3.3 V logic range.

```cpp
#include <DHT.h>

const int DHT_PIN = 4;
const int LED_PIN = 23;
#define DHT_TYPE DHT22

DHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT_TYPE);

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  dht.begin();
}

void loop() {
  float temperatureC = dht.readTemperature();

  if (isnan(temperatureC)) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, temperatureC >= 30.0 ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

  delay(2000);
}
```

The DHT22 should not be polled rapidly; a 2-second interval is appropriate for this sensor.

Wiring:
- DHT22 module VCC -> ESP32 3V3.
- DHT22 module DATA -> GPIO5.
- DHT22 module GND -> ESP32 GND.
- GPIO19 -> 330 ohm resistor -> blue LED anode.
- LED cathode -> GND.

No buzzer, display, relay, additional LED, or network service is needed.

```cpp
#include <DHT.h>

#define DHT_PIN 5
#define DHT_TYPE DHT22

const int LED_PIN = 19;
DHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT_TYPE);

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  dht.begin();
}

void loop() {
  float humidity = dht.readHumidity();

  if (isnan(humidity)) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  } else {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, humidity > 70.0 ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

  delay(2000);
}
```

The LED is current-limited by the 330 ohm series resistor, and the DHT22 module remains entirely on 3.3 V logic.

Wiring:
- DHT22 module VCC -> ESP32 3V3.
- DHT22 module DATA -> GPIO32.
- DHT22 module GND -> ESP32 GND.
- GPIO33 -> 220 ohm resistor -> green LED anode.
- LED cathode -> ESP32 GND.

Because this is a breakout module with its onboard DATA pull-up, no additional pull-up resistor is needed. Keep the module on 3.3 V so the DATA high level stays within the ESP32's 3.3 V logic range.

```cpp
#include <DHT.h>

#define DHT_PIN 32
#define DHT_TYPE DHT22

const int LED_PIN = 33;
DHT dht(DHT_PIN, DHT_TYPE);

void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_PIN, OUTPUT);
  digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  dht.begin();
}

void loop() {
  float temperatureC = dht.readTemperature();
  float humidity = dht.readHumidity();

  if (isnan(temperatureC) || isnan(humidity)) {
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, LOW);
  } else {
    bool warning = temperatureC >= 31.0 || humidity >= 75.0;
    digitalWrite(LED_PIN, warning ? HIGH : LOW);
  }

  delay(2000);
}
```

The LED condition uses logical OR exactly as requested: either threshold is enough to turn it on. A failed DHT22 read forces the LED off.
